A||||5||Shiner||Vacuum hose to Nowhere?!? Help!||||5||
Z||000000||Shiner||10-03-2001||11:30 AM||||While changing the rear spark plugs on my '87 GT, I noticed a tube that was not connected to anything.  The loose end wants to sit right between the two spark plugs, and the other end apears to go into the air cleaner assy.  No, it's not the tube that goes into the rocker cover; that is in place.  Does somebody know where this thing goes or what?  I couldn't feel anything obvious, and the archives were no help.<P>TIA!<P>------------------<BR>-Shiner-<BR>'84 Sport Coupe<BR>'87 GT<BR>'86 SE 2M6 (parts car)||||reg||5
Z||000001||btoth||10-03-2001||11:58 AM||||Is it the line that is supposed to attach to the black vacuum thingy?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.bw.edu/~btoth/me/btoth_vac(small).jpg">  <P>The rubber line that goes from it to the metal line that is up against the firewall had fallen apart into two pieces when I got the car... replaced with hose from NAPA.<P>BTW, does anyone know if it really matters if the line is connected or not?  Does it screw up the vacuum system?<p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 10-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rick 88||10-03-2001||12:22 PM||||Shiner,<P>The tube you are talking about is the one that was disconnected for the recall. Unless you have reinstalled it, the rubber seal at the back of the decklid shold have been removed as well. <P>The tube was a valve cover breather that pulled air from the filter canister to the rear valve cover. You now have a black metal tube running from the rear valve cover to the black rubber air tube between the filter and throttle body doing the same thing. <P>My old pipe has a rubber cap sealing off the open end. I understand the recall was done differently by each mechanic. You may find this rubber cap attached directly to the filter canister leaving the pipe open behind the motor.<P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shiner||10-03-2001||01:08 PM||||Damn, that was quick!  Btoth, thanks for the help, but I think you are talking about a different tube.  88, thanks for the help!<P>Thanks a ton guys!||||reg||10
Z||000004||Mark||10-03-2001||05:55 PM||||To avoid sucking unfiltered air, you really should cap that tube, if it isn't already capped. That tube draws air from the outside of the filter element, which, in the Fiero, is the engine (filtered) side!||||reg||1
Z||000005||btoth||10-03-2001||09:06 PM||||OK, I know what you're talking about now.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I think I just have a hole in the side of my filter container.||||reg||1
